---
/**
 * /guides/getting-started - the "your first 30 hours in MSCW" orientation
 * page. Top-of-funnel content for players who Google "how to start
 * maplestory classic". Structured so every section links deeper into
 * the site (jobs, party quests, character customizer, glossary).
 */

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Getting Started - Liven's Classic Maple Guide"
	description="Your first 30 hours in MapleStory Classic World: Maple Island tutorial, class choice, 1st job advancement, Kerning PQ at Lv 21, common mistakes to skip."
	currentPath="/guides"
	theme="henesys"
	decorations={false}
>
	<section class="section-heading">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow eyebrow--guide">Orientation</span>
			<h1>Getting Started</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Your first 30 hours in MapleStory Classic World, distilled. If
				you've never played v83-era Maple - or you played 15 years ago
				and forgot most of it - this is the sequence. Every section
				links to a deeper page.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose">
		<h2 id="what-is-mscw">What is Classic World, exactly?</h2>
		<p>
			MapleStory Classic World (MSCW) is Nexon's official re-release of
			MapleStory as it existed around 2007 - the "v83" era that most
			longtime players remember as the sweet spot before the Big Bang
			revamp. Explorer classes only (Warrior, Magician, Bowman, Thief).
			No Cygnus Knights, no Aran, no Legends. Four regions - Victoria
			Island, Ossyria (partial), Ludibrium, and events - with the same
			skill trees, quests, and mob rosters you probably remember.
		</p>
		<p>
			The difference: everything runs on Nexon's modern client, servers
			are officially supported (no more private-server drama), and the
			cash shop / progression have been rebalanced for a re-release
			audience. Founder's Access starts <strong>October 6</strong> with
			public launch shortly after.
		</p>
		<p>
			Not sure this matches your memory? Check the <a href="/guides/glossary">glossary</a>
			for any term that sounds unfamiliar.
		</p>

		<h2 id="maple-island">Step 1: Maple Island (Lv 1-10)</h2>
		<p>
			Every character starts on Maple Island as a Beginner. The tutorial
			zone teaches basic movement, monster kills, and the quest UI. You
			cannot leave Maple Island until you reach Lv 10 <em>and</em> finish
			the ferry quest from Shanks - so don't waste time trying.
		</p>
		<p>Priorities on the island:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Kill snails and shrooms until Lv 8-10.</strong> Green
				Snails at 1, Blue Snails around 3, Red Snails around 5, Orange
				Mushrooms at 6-10.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Do the Amos the Strong quest chain.</strong> Free
				beginner armor + a Maple weapon that carries you into Victoria
				Island. Talk to every NPC in Amherst / Southperry once.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Bank your first meso via Storage.</strong> Storage NPCs
				share inventory across your account - useful once you make a
				mule later.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Don't buy potions yet.</strong> Shroom Souls and
				beginner-tier consumables from quest rewards are enough. Save
				meso for after you leave.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Full Maple Island detail lives on the <a href="/world">world map</a>
			- click the Maple Island pin.
		</p>

		<h2 id="choosing-class">Step 2: Choose your class</h2>
		<p>
			At Lv 10 (or Lv 8 for Magicians), you visit a class instructor to
			advance from Beginner. Each class lives in a different town and
			suits a different playstyle.
		</p>
		<div class="class-grid">
			<a class="class-card class-card--warrior" href="/jobs/warrior">
				<h3>Warrior <span>Perion</span></h3>
				<p>
					High HP, melee, STR-based. Fighter (Sword/Axe), Page
					(Sword/BW), or Spearman (Spear/Polearm) branches at Lv 30.
					Great party support (Rage, Hyper Body). Slowest solo
					early-game but tanky forever.
				</p>
			</a>
			<a class="class-card class-card--magician" href="/jobs/magician">
				<h3>Magician <span>Ellinia</span></h3>
				<p>
					Fastest early-game leveling thanks to Magic Claw + Magic
					Guard. F.P./I.L. Wizard (nukes) or Cleric (heals + undead
					damage) at Lv 30. Squishy but scales into endgame nukes.
					<em>Advances at Lv 8, not 10.</em>
				</p>
			</a>
			<a class="class-card class-card--bowman" href="/jobs/bowman">
				<h3>Bowman <span>Henesys</span></h3>
				<p>
					Ranged DEX-based DPS. Hunter (Bow) or Crossbowman (Xbow) at
					Lv 30. Consistent damage, high crit, becomes the boss-carry
					class at 4th job with Sharp Eyes.
				</p>
			</a>
			<a class="class-card class-card--thief" href="/jobs/thief">
				<h3>Thief <span>Kerning City</span></h3>
				<p>
					LUK-based. Assassin (throwing stars) or Bandit (daggers) at
					Lv 30. Fast attacks, high avoidability. Bandit gets Meso
					Explosion at 3rd job - the class's signature meme skill.
				</p>
			</a>
		</div>
		<p class="callout">
			<strong>Can't decide?</strong> Magician is the newbie-friendliest -
			easy leveling, useful party buffs, forgiving skill build. Warrior
			is the most beginner-hostile solo (slow, expensive potions) but
			gets invited to every party. Play what looks fun; every class ships
			with 2-4 <a href="/jobs">skill builds</a> here.
		</p>

		<h2 id="first-job">Step 3: First Job Advancement</h2>
		<p>
			Once you hit Lv 10 (Magicians: Lv 8), take the boat/portal to your
			class's home town and visit the instructor. Advancement is free and
			hands you 1 SP to spend immediately, plus 3 SP per level after that.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Spend your first SP the moment you advance.</strong> Every
			class ships with recommended 1st-job builds on the <a href="/jobs">Jobs</a>
			pages. Warriors: Improve MaxHP + Slash Blast. Magicians: Magic
			Guard THEN Magic Claw. Bowmen: Double Shot or Arrow Blow. Thieves:
			Lucky Seven (Assassin) or Double Stab (Bandit). Do not skip these
			- they define your training speed.
		</p>

		<h2 id="lv-11-30">Step 4: Grinding Lv 11-30</h2>
		<p>
			The gap from 1st job to 2nd job is where new players quit. It's
			slow, repetitive, and most classes hit a wall around Lv 25-28. The
			antidote is <strong>Kerning Party Quest at Lv 21</strong>.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/party-quests/kerning-pq">Kerning PQ (KPQ)</a> is a 3-6
			player instanced dungeon with puzzles, mob waves, and a mini-boss.
			Gives 2-3x the EXP-per-hour of solo grinding, drops Rock/Paper/Scissors
			gloves that carry your 1st job, and teaches party mechanics you'll
			need for LPQ at Lv 35.
		</p>
		<p>
			Between PQ runs, grind at:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Lv 11-15:</strong> Stumps and Slimes on the Perion/Henesys/Ellinia border maps</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 15-20:</strong> Green Mushrooms in Mushroom Park (Henesys)</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 20-25:</strong> Ligators / Croco in Kerning Swamp; Wild Boars in Perion</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 25-30:</strong> Fire Boar Ashen Land; Zombie Mushrooms in Sleepywood; Kerning PQ every hour</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="lv-30-advancement">Step 5: 2nd Job at Lv 30</h2>
		<p>
			At Lv 30 (all classes), you unlock the 2nd job advancement quest -
			a short combat trial + fetch quest from your class instructor.
			Warriors branch into Fighter/Page/Spearman, Bowmen into
			Hunter/Crossbowman, etc.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Plan your 2nd-job build BEFORE you hit Lv 30.</strong>
			You'll have 121 SP to spend from Lv 30-70 and misallocating early
			levels hurts. Every 2nd-job class has both a canonical build and an
			alt build on the <a href="/jobs">Jobs</a> pages - pick one, then
			follow the per-level SP tables.
		</p>

		<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes to skip</h2>
		<ul class="mistakes">
			<li>
				<strong>Selling unfamiliar items.</strong> Some quest items
				don't get flagged clearly. When in doubt, keep it. Detailed
				list coming in a future guide - for now, if it has "Ore",
				"Scroll", "Coin", "Card", or a class name in the item name,
				don't sell.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Blowing meso on potions.</strong> Under Lv 30, cheap
				potions (Red Potion, Orange Potion) are all you need. Save meso
				for a job-change weapon at 30 and gear rebuys around 35.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Scrolling gear you're about to outgrow.</strong> Do not
				scroll anything you plan to replace inside 10 levels. Scrolls
				are permanent to the item - if the item goes into storage or
				trade, the meso is gone with it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Skipping stat allocation.</strong> Every class has a
				stat build rule (usually 4/1 - four points into primary, one
				into secondary). Random-allocating AP will brick your class by
				Lv 30. Check the class page.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Ignoring party invites.</strong> Party EXP shares
				generously in Classic - 3-player parties often out-EXP solo.
				Say yes to friendly invites in your training map's channel.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Solo grinding at KPQ level.</strong> If you're between
				Lv 21 and Lv 30 and NOT running KPQ every hour, you're leaving
				2-3x EXP on the table. It's the best content for that range.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="whats-next">Where to go next</h2>
		<ul class="next-links">
			<li><a href="/guides/glossary">Glossary</a> - 80+ terms explained</li>
			<li><a href="/guides/meso-saving">Meso Saving Guide</a> - where your first million goes</li>
			<li><a href="/jobs">Jobs & Skill Builds</a> - canonical + alt builds for every class</li>
			<li><a href="/party-quests/kerning-pq">KPQ Walkthrough</a> - the Lv 21-30 grind saviour</li>
			<li><a href="/bosses">Boss Compendium</a> - what to fight after you hit 2nd job</li>
			<li><a href="/character">Character Customizer</a> - preview hair/face/NX equips</li>
		</ul>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.section-heading {
		max-width: 68ch;
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}

	.prose {
		max-width: 72ch;
		color: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	.prose h2 {
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem;
		padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
	}
	.prose p {
		margin: 0.7rem 0;
	}
	.prose ul {
		margin: 0.7rem 0;
		padding-left: 1.4rem;
	}
	.prose li {
		margin: 0.35rem 0;
	}
	.prose a {
		color: var(--accent-primary);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.class-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
		gap: 0.7rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
	}
	.class-card {
		display: block;
		padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
		background: var(--surface-card);
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		border-left-width: 4px;
		border-radius: 0.4rem;
		text-decoration: none;
		color: inherit;
		transition: transform 0.14s ease, border-color 0.14s ease;
	}
	.class-card:hover {
		transform: translateY(-2px);
		border-color: var(--accent-primary);
	}
	.class-card h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		align-items: baseline;
		gap: 0.6rem;
	}
	.class-card h3 span {
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		font-weight: 500;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.05em;
	}
	.class-card p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.45;
	}
	.class-card--warrior { border-left-color: #c94a4a; }
	.class-card--magician { border-left-color: #7a4bc7; }
	.class-card--bowman { border-left-color: #3ea86b; }
	.class-card--thief { border-left-color: #4d6674; }

	.callout {
		margin: 1.2rem 0 !important;
		padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
		background: var(--bg-cloud);
		border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary);
		border-radius: 0.35rem;
	}

	.mistakes li strong {
		color: var(--text-heading);
	}

	.next-links {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0 !important;
	}
	.next-links li {
		padding: 0.5rem 0.8rem;
		background: var(--surface-card);
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		border-radius: 0.35rem;
		margin: 0.35rem 0 !important;
	}
</style>
